[50.866425, "o", "\u001b[2J\u001b[3J\u001b[H\r\n\r\n  Cursor Agent\r\n  \u001b[2mv2026.11.13-eacade1\u001b[22m\r\n  \u001b[2mTip: Use /plan to iterate on an implementation plan before code changes.\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n \u001b[48;2;36;36;40m                                                                                      \u001b[49m\r\n \u001b[48;2;36;36;40m I'm on the SOC team and just got an \"abnormal user behavior\" alert in Jira.          \u001b[49m\r\n \u001b[48;2;36;36;40m List the open alerts briefly and tell me which ones look related.                    \u001b[49m\r\n \u001b[48;2;36;36;40m                                                                                      \u001b[49m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[37m\u001b[2m \u001b[22m\u001b[39m \u001b[1mExplored\u001b[22m  \u001b[2mpattern getAccessibleAtlassianResources|searchJiraIssuesUsingJql|getJiraIssue$\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ma\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mv\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ma\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mi\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ml\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ma\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mb\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ml\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2me\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n             \u001b[2mM\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mC\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mP\u001b[22m\r"]
[50.866436, "o", "\r\n\r\n             \u001b[2mt\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mo\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mo\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ml\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ms\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[37m\u001b[2m \u001b[22m\u001b[39m \u001b[1matlassian\u001b[22m \u001b[2mgetAccessibleAtlassianResources\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[37m\u001b[2m \u001b[22m\u001b[39m \u001b[1matlassian\u001b[22m \u001b[2msearchJiraIssuesUsingJql\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n  Six open alerts in SOC (all in Backlog):\r\n\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-1\u001b[22m (High): Abnormal user behavior: spike in declined card payments,\r\n    concentrated on Northwind Electronics and Contoso Gift Cards.\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-2\u001b[22m (High): Brute-force login attempts from external IPs against\r\n    online-banking accounts, some followed by a successful login.\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-3\u001b[22m (Medium): Partner merchants Northwind and Contoso report bursts of small\r\n    charges that look like card testing.\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-4\u001b[22m (Medium): Unusual session activity: some accounts may be transacting\r\n    without logging in.\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-5\u001b[22m (Low): Settlement returns slightly above normal for partner"]
[50.866448, "o", " merchants.\r\n    The data has no merchant_name field.\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-6\u001b[22m (Low): Printer offline on floor 3. Not security-related.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[1mLikely related:\u001b[22m\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-1, SOC-3\u001b[22m are the same activity: same two merchants, same window, and\r\n    card-testing bursts would explain the declines.\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-2, SOC-4\u001b[22m look connected: brute-force logins with some successes, followed\r\n    by sessions transacting without login, suggests possible account takeover.\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-1 and SOC-2\u001b[22m may be linked too, if the compromised accounts are what's\r\n    being used for the declined card payments. Splunk would confirm this.\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-5\u001b[22m may be a weak link (same partners, but no merchant field to tie it to\r\n    the others).\r\n  \u001b[2m•\u001b[22m \u001b[1mSOC-6\u001b[22m is unrelated.\r\n\r\n  This is based on the Jira descriptions only. I haven't queried Splunk yet. Want me\r\n  to check whether the SOC-2 accounts overlap with the SOC-1 declines?\r\n\r\n \u001b[48;2;36;36;40m"]
[50.866479, "o", "                                                                                      \u001b[49m\r\n \u001b[48;2;36;36;40m Run the ML models in our saved searches against that Splunk data. For each           \u001b[49m\r\n \u001b[48;2;36;36;40m one, say which model it applies and show the top rows it flags.                      \u001b[49m\r\n \u001b[48;2;36;36;40m                                                                                      \u001b[49m\r\n\r\n  I'll list our \u001b[38;2;168;181;230mdemo_*\u001b[39m ML saved searches in Splunk, then run each one and report\r\n  what it flags.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[37m\u001b[2m \u001b[22m\u001b[39m \u001b[1mExplored\u001b[22m  \u001b[2mpattern splunk_run_query|splunk_run_saved_search|splunk_get_knowledge_objects\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ma\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mv\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ma\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mi\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ml\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ma\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mb\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ml\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2me\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n             \u001b[2mM\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mC\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mP\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n             \u001b[2mt\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b"]
[50.866489, "o", "[2mo\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2mo\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ml\u001b[22m\r\n             \u001b[2ms\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[33m \u001b[39m \u001b[1mUsing\u001b[22m \u001b[2msplunkctl\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n \u001b[32m⠠⠛\u001b[39m \u001b[1mReading\u001b[22m \u001b[2m 218 tokens\u001b[22m\r\n\r\n \u001b[48;2;21;21;21m                                                                                      \u001b[49m\r\n \u001b[48;2;21;21;21m \u001b[2m→ \u001b[22m\u001b[7mA\u001b[27m\u001b[2mdd a follow-up\u001b[22m                                                     \u001b[2mctrl+c to stop\u001b[22m \u001b[49m\r\n \u001b[48;2;21;21;21m                                                                                      \u001b[49m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[2mAuto Balance\u001b[22m \u001b[2m·\u001b[22m \u001b[2m10.9%\u001b[22m                                                  \u001b[35mRun Everything\u001b[39m\r\n  \u001b[2m~/payments\u001b[22m\r\n"]
